00:13:27.040 --> 00:13:31.040
just be linked back to the fundamentals of how&nbsp;
molecules interact with each other things like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.040 --> 00:13:35.680
why you get condensation on the outside of cold&nbsp;
drink why raindrops merge on a window why you get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.680 --> 00:13:40.640
bubbles from detergent why if you have dry pencil&nbsp;
lead it makes your hand dirty whereas like if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.640 --> 00:13:46.400
have dry pen ink it doesn't how like different&nbsp;
painkillers worked so i went to a really academic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.400 --> 00:13:52.000
high school and we had these lunchtime chemistry&nbsp;
classes and my friend went to it so i tagged along&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:52.000 --> 00:13:56.240
it was sort of like more of a social event for&nbsp;
me here's something that i think might be a bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.240 --> 00:14:00.560
unexpected i don't think i started off naturally&nbsp;
good at chemistry but because i went to all these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.560 --> 00:14:05.760
extra lunchtime classes mostly to hang out with&nbsp;
my friends i got a lot of practice and then i&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.760 --> 00:14:10.320
got good at chemistry i think that's actually why&nbsp;
i'm good at teaching chemistry because i have an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.320 --> 00:14:15.280
insight into what it's like to suck at chemistry&nbsp;
in australia there's a system where you get a mark&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:15.280 --> 00:14:19.200
at the end of high school and then different&nbsp;
uni courses have different cutoff marks and so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:19.200 --> 00:14:24.080
there's this stupid idea of not wasting your marks&nbsp;
so a lot of people in my high school get pressured&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.080 --> 00:14:28.640
into doing medicine or law and so i picked&nbsp;
combined law at sydney uni at the time it had the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.640 --> 00:14:33.680
highest entry mark so i wasted less of my marks&nbsp;
and a lot of my friends were doing law it seemed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:33.680 --> 00:14:38.720
like a respectable job and it's the top law school&nbsp;
in the country i combined law with science for two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:38.720 --> 00:14:43.600
reasons the main one was when i was 17 science&nbsp;
sounded like something that would be a bit more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:43.600 --> 00:14:48.480
of a useful degree than arts also because i went&nbsp;
to those lunchtime classes i ended up being top&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.480 --> 00:14:52.800
20 in the state for chemistry olympiad and that&nbsp;
meant you could actually skip first year chemistry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:52.800 --> 00:14:57.200
so basically my approach was i picked subjects i&nbsp;
was somewhat interested in and potentially had a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:57.200 --> 00:15:03.040
career after it and if i could get higher marks&nbsp;
without as much effort then even better so my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:03.040 --> 00:15:08.160
science courses were chemistry psychology maths&nbsp;
pharmacology and physiology so the idea was i&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:08.160 --> 00:15:13.280
could become a patent attorney or potentially go&nbsp;
into drug design i was really fascinated by how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:13.280 --> 00:15:17.200
different substances could have like really&nbsp;
different effects on your body so different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:17.200 --> 00:15:22.320
things could get you to stop sneezing or stop your&nbsp;
headache or hallucinate or drop dead um my parents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:22.320 --> 00:15:26.720
are nurses so when i was a kid i got a lot of&nbsp;
random stuff given to me when i was sick so after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:26.720 --> 00:15:31.120
the first three years of undergrad i paused law&nbsp;
for a year to do honours in chemistry i ended up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:31.120 --> 00:15:36.400
dropping law because even though i love studying&nbsp;
law it turns out i hated working in a law firm the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.400 --> 00:15:42.640
project i was working on was where my law firm was&nbsp;
representing a tobacco company and i never really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:42.640 --> 00:15:48.160
considered myself a super moral person i assumed&nbsp;
if i got paid enough then i would be comfortable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:48.160 --> 00:15:53.440
giving fair representation to any party because&nbsp;
that's what all lawyers should be doing but it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:53.440 --> 00:15:57.840
just slowly ate away at me that i was working&nbsp;
so hard and the harder i was working the more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:57.840 --> 00:16:02.800
i was helping what to me seem like the bad guys&nbsp;
win there's no judgment for me for people who do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:02.800 --> 00:16:07.200
this kind of work i have a lot of friends in law&nbsp;
and i think detachment is a really useful skill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:07.200 --> 00:16:13.040
but it just isn't the way i'm built so because of&nbsp;
that experience i'm a big believer in trying out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:13.040 --> 00:16:17.120
lots of different things if you think you might&nbsp;
be interested in something then just try it out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:17.120 --> 00:16:21.520
because you don't really know what it's like&nbsp;
unless you immerse yourself in that experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:21.520 --> 00:16:25.360
if you can't get practical hands-on experience&nbsp;
then try to talk to people who work in the fields&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:25.360 --> 00:16:29.360
that you might want to go into so hopefully you&nbsp;
get a better feel for what actually interests you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:29.360 --> 00:16:33.920
and what you enjoy doing and whether or not that&nbsp;
field matches that so i discovered that a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:33.920 --> 00:16:38.960
people become patent attorneys after getting a phd&nbsp;
so doing a phd seemed like a good way to keep both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:38.960 --> 00:16:44.800
of those patent attorneys drug design parts open&nbsp;
my phd was a continuation of my honours project&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:44.800 --> 00:16:49.040
which was really interesting it was on medicinal&nbsp;
and supramolecular chemistry i also made a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:49.040 --> 00:16:53.360
friends with my cohort and a lot of us decided&nbsp;
to continue our phd so that was a really nice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:53.360 --> 00:16:57.120
group of people to be around there was also&nbsp;
the fact that the admin to change unis let&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:57.120 --> 00:17:02.880
alone countries was just a lot i'm really bad with&nbsp;
dealing with admin and staying at sydney uni was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:02.880 --> 00:17:09.040
just like filling in two pages so that's the not&nbsp;
very inspiring story of how i ended up doing a phd

00:17:09.040 --> 00:17:13.440
what made you decide to go the scicomm route and&nbsp;
not stay in academia i've always really enjoyed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:13.440 --> 00:17:18.880
teaching so during my phd i did a bit of lab&nbsp;
teaching and i also taught tutorials to undergrads&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:18.880 --> 00:17:23.760
i also did a lot of tutoring before this so my&nbsp;
first tutoring job was at 14 and when i was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:23.760 --> 00:17:28.720
kid i actually remember inventing multiple choice&nbsp;
tests for my friends to do so that they could join&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:28.720 --> 00:17:34.640
a club yeah i was just a really annoying child so&nbsp;
there's just not that much teaching in academia a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:34.640 --> 00:17:40.480
lot of academia just revolves around research but&nbsp;
a lot of scicomm is pretty much like teaching so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:40.480 --> 00:17:44.560
that's another reason why i started lab muffin&nbsp;
at the time people told you that if you wanted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:44.560 --> 00:17:49.200
to go into scicomm you should start a blog as&nbsp;
like a portfolio and it could really show that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:49.200 --> 00:17:53.360
you were truly interested in scicomm so i think&nbsp;
the main thing that got me out of thinking of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:53.360 --> 00:17:58.640
academia as a long-term job was that there was a&nbsp;
fire in my lab in 2012 it was the final year of my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:58.640 --> 00:18:04.720
phd and i was in the lab where the fire was and i&nbsp;
pretty much just saw my life flash before my eyes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:04.720 --> 00:18:10.080
our labs were organized so that all the organic&nbsp;
chemists were on one level and organic chemistry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:10.080 --> 00:18:15.200
involves a lot of really flammable solvents so you&nbsp;
can basically imagine it was just a lab where on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:15.200 --> 00:18:20.400
top of every lab desk there was just a bunch of&nbsp;
petrol in my mind in that moment i thought the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:20.400 --> 00:18:26.480
whole building would just go up in flames i could&nbsp;
just see a wall of flames just like exploding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:26.480 --> 00:18:32.880
towards me it didn't the fire was actually&nbsp;
confined to a really small area but that incident&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:32.880 --> 00:18:40.240
affected me a lot i had ptsd for about six months&nbsp;
i had nightmares every single night when i went&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:40.240 --> 00:18:44.960
back near that building i got this really&nbsp;
uncomfortable like skin crawling feeling and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:44.960 --> 00:18:50.320
it made finishing my phd really difficult i didn't&nbsp;
publish as many papers as i probably could have i&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:50.320 --> 00:18:54.960
took a bit longer than i should have as well that&nbsp;
incident really highlighted that i love chemistry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:54.960 --> 00:18:59.840
but not enough to die because someone else screwed&nbsp;
up so after i graduated i didn't really know what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:59.840 --> 00:19:04.400
to do i lived in switzerland for half a year with&nbsp;
my partner at the time i got really depressed i&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:04.400 --> 00:19:08.640
worked on my blog i became a moderator on&nbsp;
/r/skincareaddiction on reddit i started&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:08.640 --> 00:19:13.120
teaching at matrix education to earn some money&nbsp;
on the side this is a tutoring college that does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:13.120 --> 00:19:18.240
usually classes of 15 they hire a lot of phd&nbsp;
students and they paid really well and about six&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:18.240 --> 00:19:22.720
months later they offered me a full-time position&nbsp;
as a chemistry coordinator so i was in charge of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:22.720 --> 00:19:28.000
hiring teachers teaching and writing the teaching&nbsp;
materials so i was writing the textbooks and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:28.000 --> 00:19:33.280
inventing experiments and so i sort of just fell&nbsp;
into that role and i found that i absolutely loved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:33.280 --> 00:19:37.920
it i stayed there until i went full time with lab&nbsp;
muffin and i still teach there a few hours a week

00:19:37.920 --> 00:19:44.240
where did the name lab muffin come from this story&nbsp;
is just not that exciting um so i knew i wanted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:44.240 --> 00:19:50.000
do a blog i couldn't think of a name and so i&nbsp;
couldn't really start doing my blog for about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:50.000 --> 00:19:54.880
four weeks and i knew i just had to start so i&nbsp;
settled on lab muffin i was sitting in a lab at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:54.880 --> 00:19:59.200
the time when i set up my first blogspot account&nbsp;
muffin was the nickname from my partner at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:59.200 --> 00:20:04.400
time i told myself i could just start and then&nbsp;
change the name later and i just never thought up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:04.400 --> 00:20:08.240
a better name and i never got around to changing&nbsp;
it so the good thing about the phrase lab muffin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:08.240 --> 00:20:12.880
is that it is like a brand new sentence which&nbsp;
means it's very search engine friendly so back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:12.880 --> 00:20:17.280
in 2012 if you typed in lab muffin into google i&nbsp;
would be the first result so if you're trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:17.280 --> 00:20:21.760
pick an online handle and you want to be very&nbsp;
easily searchable i would definitely recommend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:21.760 --> 00:20:26.560
something relatively unique and easy to spell lab&nbsp;
muffin worked really well for that if you have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:26.560 --> 00:20:31.760
really distinctive name then i would just go with&nbsp;
your name but that wasn't an option for me because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:31.760 --> 00:20:37.200
there were literally 27 other michelle wongs&nbsp;
back when i tried to sign up to hotmail in 1997

00:20:37.200 --> 00:20:42.000
how did your phd slash degree help you with your&nbsp;
career and what did you have to learn yourself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:42.000 --> 00:20:47.040
so my phd project was on cyclic peptides&nbsp;
i was making them and then exploring their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:47.040 --> 00:20:51.840
uses as drugs and supramolecular scaffolds so&nbsp;
that combined with my undergrad subjects gave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:51.840 --> 00:20:56.320
me a really good background understanding of&nbsp;
things like chemistry pharmacology physiology&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:56.320 --> 00:21:00.640
and medicinal chemistry so from that i was&nbsp;
really used to looking at things like ligands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:00.640 --> 00:21:06.320
and biochemical pathways but i never really&nbsp;
looked at anything like retinoids specifically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:06.320 --> 00:21:11.040
so i think more importantly i developed a lot&nbsp;
of other skills from my phd training and from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:11.040 --> 00:21:15.840
my previous job during a typical phd you have&nbsp;
to defend your ideas against people who are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:16.880 --> 00:21:21.520
kind of trying to make you break it's a bit toxic&nbsp;
some people cry but you kind of get used to having&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:21.520 --> 00:21:27.120
to really critique your own ideas so that you can&nbsp;
work out all the flaws before you go up on stage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:27.120 --> 00:21:32.080
and have to publicly defend yourself writing&nbsp;
the textbooks was also really good training&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:32.080 --> 00:21:37.280
because the audience was both chemistry&nbsp;
phds and phd students who are teaching it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:37.280 --> 00:21:41.600
and the high school students so you had to&nbsp;
make it work for a lot of different people

00:21:41.600 --> 00:21:47.440
when did you realize you could go full time&nbsp;
as a science communicator so in 2017 i added&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:47.440 --> 00:21:52.800
banner ads to my blog and that's when financially&nbsp;
i started making more money than i was spending on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:52.800 --> 00:21:57.040
lab muffin and that's when it started looking&nbsp;
like it could potentially be a part-time job&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:57.040 --> 00:22:02.720
i was starting to get over a thousand dollars a&nbsp;
month without a ton of effort and i really wish i&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:02.720 --> 00:22:08.160
discovered this a lot earlier on and not six years&nbsp;
in so yeah i guess my advice would be if there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:08.160 --> 00:22:13.520
a hobby that you love doing see if you can make&nbsp;
money out of it earlier on in late 2017 i started&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:13.520 --> 00:22:18.080
my youtube channel and that's when i started to&nbsp;
get a lot more sponsorship opportunities and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:18.080 --> 00:22:23.440
workload started to really ramp up by late 2018&nbsp;
i was working on lab muffin on the train to and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:23.440 --> 00:22:29.360
from work and every weekend i pretty much had&nbsp;
no life i worked like 70 to 80 hours a week&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:29.360 --> 00:22:33.760
i was basically in lockdown before lockdown was&nbsp;
a thing my accountant didn't actually believe how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:33.760 --> 00:22:38.640
many hours i worked until i showed her my records&nbsp;
every sunday monday which was my weekend i'd&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:38.640 --> 00:22:44.080
literally get out of bed stumble across the room&nbsp;
to my chair and then work straight from 9am to 1am&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:44.080 --> 00:22:48.640
i went out of my room for meals and for bathroom&nbsp;
breaks and that was it i do not recommend this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:48.640 --> 00:22:53.680
at all i recommend trying to figure out a way to&nbsp;
avoid this as early as you can by the middle of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:53.680 --> 00:22:58.240
2019 i was making enough money from content&nbsp;
creation that it could be a full-time job&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:58.240 --> 00:23:03.280
and i was just about to burn out from working&nbsp;
every single waking moment i was constantly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:03.280 --> 00:23:08.080
shallow breathing i had a racing heart my memory&nbsp;
was getting really bad and i was starting to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:08.080 --> 00:23:13.520
these like weird feelings in my legs like i&nbsp;
was getting little clots so i resigned um i'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:13.520 --> 00:23:18.240
still not really good at that whole work-life&nbsp;
balance thing but it has gotten a lot better

00:23:18.240 --> 00:23:23.600
so starting out and staying motivated i think one&nbsp;
of the things that really kept me going was having&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:23.600 --> 00:23:28.240
a nice community around me there were a lot of&nbsp;
other bloggers back in the day and we all used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:28.240 --> 00:23:32.880
to comment on each other's blogs not in like an&nbsp;
organized way but just being really supportive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:32.880 --> 00:23:36.000
there wasn't like an algorithm&nbsp;
back then so we weren't really like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:36.000 --> 00:23:41.040
boosting each other's posts like you might&nbsp;
on instagram it was just like a really nice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:41.040 --> 00:23:45.600
supportive environment there was also this nail&nbsp;
